ut suffers the same unbalanced-reaction defects as other hydrolytic cyclization variants.', 'Wet-dry variant with ammonia recycling has similar structural and feasibility problems as other multi-cyclization routes. Multiple atom/charge imbalances limit chemical credibility.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"L-Arginine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"We construct a complete prebiotic synthesis network for L-arginine, exploiting the experimentally established cyanosulfidic protometabolism as documented by Patel et al. (2015, Nature Chemistry), and including all three literature-supported cyclization routes (dry, hydrolytic, hydrolytic with NH3 release). All reactions are explicitly mass- and atom-balanced, with all small molecule inputs/byproducts enumerated and tracked. Each pathway begins from simple molecules\u2014acetylene, hydrogen cyanide, ammonia, cyanamide, hydrogen sulfide, and water\u2014under surface (cyanosulfidic) conditions, and proceeds through acrylonitrile, aminopropanenitrile, key guanidino/nitrile intermediates, and ring/thioamide/cyanohydrin transformations, leading to arginine via a final Strecker-like nitrile hydrolysis. No step violates atom conservation, and all documented variants that afford mechanistically distinct and balanced outcomes are included, for a total of 10 convergent, mass-consistent routes.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2/c1-2/h1-2H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CHN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",